---
// The explanation below the Lab: the data, the symbols, how Naive Bayes
// decides, smoothing, the training messages, and the model in short. Static
// HTML; formulas are rendered by KaTeX at build time.
import type { Dataset } from './data';

import { renderTex } from '../../lib/katex';
import { equations } from './equations';

interface Props {
  dataset: Dataset;
}

const { dataset } = Astro.props;
const tex = Object.fromEntries(equations.map((e) => [e.id, renderTex(e.tex)]));
const spam = dataset.training.filter((m) => m.label === 'spam');
const normal = dataset.training.filter((m) => m.label === 'normal');
---

<div class="explain">
  <section aria-labelledby="nb-data">
    <h2 id="nb-data">What you are looking at</h2>
    <p>
      The model has read {dataset.training.length} short messages that someone
      already sorted: {spam.length} spam and {normal.length} normal. From them
      it learned how often each word turns up in each kind of message. Now it
      reads a new message and weighs every word.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each bar is one word of the message. A bar to the right means the word is
      more common in spam; to the left, more common in normal messages. The
      longer the bar, the stronger the evidence. A word the model never saw
      gives no evidence at all, so it is ignored. The messages are made up, in
      simple words, so every count is easy to check.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="nb-symbols">
    <h2 id="nb-symbols">What each symbol means</h2>
    <dl class="symbols">
      <div>
        <dt>w</dt>
        <dd>One word of the message.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>P(w | spam)</dt>
        <dd>
          How likely a spam message is to use the word w. Read the bar "|" as
          "given".
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>P(spam)</dt>
        <dd>
          How common spam is before reading anything: here {spam.length} of{' '}
          {dataset.training.length} messages.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-line">L</dt>
        <dd>
          The total evidence. Above zero points to spam; below zero, to normal.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt class="sym-a">α</dt>
        <dd>Smoothing: a small extra count given to every word.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>V</dt>
        <dd>The number of different words seen in training.</dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="nb-how">
    <h2 id="nb-how">How the model decides</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Start</strong> from how common each kind is. Most messages here
        are normal, so before reading a single word the model leans a little
        towards normal. That is the first bar.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Weigh each word</strong> by comparing how likely it is in spam
        and in normal messages. "Free" turns up in many spam messages and in no
        normal ones, so it points strongly to spam.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.word} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Add up</strong> the evidence. Taking the log of each ratio turns
        multiplying chances into adding bars, which is easier to see and to
        compute.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.odds} />
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Turn the total into a chance</strong> between 0% and 100%. A
        total of zero means 50%: the words balance out.
        <div class="formula" set:html={tex.chance} />
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="nb-naive">
    <h2 id="nb-naive">Why "naive"?</h2>
    <p>
      The model treats every word as separate evidence, as if words never went
      together. It ignores order, too: "money free win" scores the same as "win
      free money". Real language is not like that, so the assumption is naive.
      Even so, the model is fast, needs very little data, and works well for
      sorting messages.
    </p>
    <p>
      Switching a word off shows how much the answer rests on it. It is the same
      as deleting the word from the message.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="nb-alpha">
    <h2 id="nb-alpha">Smoothing, α</h2>
    <p>
      Without smoothing, a word never seen in normal messages would have a
      chance of exactly zero there. One such word would then make a message
      certain spam, however normal the rest looks. Smoothing adds α to every
      count, as if each word had been seen a little in both kinds.
    </p>
    <p>
      A small α trusts the counts: rare words give strong evidence. A large α
      pulls every word's chance towards the same value, so the bars shrink and
      the answer moves back towards where the model started.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="nb-training">
    <h2 id="nb-training">The messages it learned from</h2>
    <div class="training">
      <div>
        <h3 class="sym-class-1">Spam</h3>
        <ul>
          {spam.map((m) => (
            <li>{m.text}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3 class="sym-class-2">Normal</h3>
        <ul>
          {normal.map((m) => (
            <li>{m.text}</li>
          ))}
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="nb-summary">
    <h2 id="nb-summary">The model in short</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>
        <strong>Count</strong> every word in messages that are already sorted.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Smooth</strong> the counts, so no word is ever impossible.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Weigh</strong> each word of a new message: is it likelier in
        spam or in normal messages?
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Add</strong> the evidence to the starting point, and turn the
        total into a chance.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>
</div>

<style>
  .explain {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    max-width: 68ch;
    font-size: var(--fs-16);
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  section {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-4);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: var(--fs-24);
  }

  h3 {
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .formula {
    overflow-x: auto;
    padding-block: var(--s-2);
    font-size: var(--fs-18);
  }

  .symbols {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
  }

  .symbols div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 8rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    align-items: baseline;
  }

  .symbols dt {
    font-family: 'KaTeX_Math', 'Times New Roman', serif;
    font-size: var(--fs-20);
    font-style: italic;
  }

  .symbols dd {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .steps {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-3);
    margin: 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.4em;
  }

  .training {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(14rem, 1fr));
    gap: var(--s-6);
  }

  .training ul {
    margin: var(--s-2) 0 0;
    padding-inline-start: 1.2em;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }
</style>
